This program presents a physics-informed GAN architecture for the Zermelo Navigation problem that integrates both Hamiltonian dynamics and heading angle constraints into the training process.
The generator outputs 7-dimensional trajectories (175-dimensional flattened vectors), and additionally computes the Hamiltonian and heading angle at each discretized point along the path. 
These physics-based quantities are used to penalize deviations from optimal behavior, ensuring physically plausible and directionally consistent outputs. 
The discriminator evaluates only a subset of each trajectory (first 50 features) to distinguish real from generated samples. 
Training combines adversarial loss with Hamiltonian and heading angle regularization, using 4000 expert trajectories as reference.
Post training, the model can generate 1000 physically consistent, time-optimal trajectories which are saved to disk for further analysis or downstream control tasks.
